/* Assessment Guide – 19 steps. Teaching text is original; guideline points cite the clause checked.
   w = interactive widgets for the step; dic = "Does it count?" items; q = check questions. */
window.AGSTEPS = [
{n:1,id:'referral',t:'Referral and triage',s:'Who should be referred, by which route, and what needs a different pathway first.',
 vis:{svg:'referral'},
 teach:'<p>Assessment starts before you meet the person. Read the referral and ask three questions: <strong>is this the right service, is it the right time, and is anything urgent?</strong></p>\
<h4>Children and young people (NG87 1.2.4\u20131.2.9)</h4><ul><li>If a child shows behaviour suggestive of ADHD, primary care judges how severe the problems are, how they affect the child or young person and their family, and how pervasive they are across settings (1.2.6).</li><li>If the problems have an adverse impact on the child’s development or family life, consider <strong>watchful waiting for up to 10 weeks</strong> and offer group-based parent support; refer to secondary care if problems persist with at least moderate impairment (1.2.7).</li><li>If impairment is <strong>severe</strong>, refer directly to secondary care (1.2.8).</li><li>Primary care should <strong>not</strong> diagnose ADHD or start ADHD medication (1.2.9).</li></ul>\
<h4>Adults (NG87 1.2.10\u20131.2.11)</h4><ul><li>Refer for assessment if symptoms began in childhood and have persisted, are not explained by another diagnosis, and cause at least moderate psychological, social, educational or occupational impairment (1.2.10).</li><li>Adults previously treated for ADHD as children who now need ongoing treatment should be referred to general adult psychiatric services (1.2.11).</li></ul>\
<h4>High-prevalence groups (NG87 1.2.1)</h4><p>Be alert to people born preterm, looked-after children, people with ODD or conduct disorder, mood disorders, a close relative with ADHD, epilepsy, other neurodevelopmental conditions (autism, tics, learning disability), a mental health condition in adults, a history of substance misuse, people in the youth or adult justice system, and acquired brain injury. Girls and women are under-recognised (1.2.2).</p>\
<h4>Red flags that need a different response first</h4><p>Some referrals need urgent action before (or instead of) an ADHD assessment: active suicidal thoughts or self-harm, possible psychosis or mania, a safeguarding concern, loss of previously acquired skills (regression), suspected seizures, or new neurological signs. <span class="badge unv">Clinical practice, not NG87 text</span></p>',
 chk:['Referral question clear (what is being asked?)','Route correct for age (NG87 1.2.6\u20131.2.11)','Childhood onset suggested in the letter','Impairment at least moderate','Red flags screened (risk, psychosis/mania, safeguarding, regression, seizures)','High-prevalence group noted (NG87 1.2.1)'],
 w:['triage'],
 q:[{q:'A GP wants to "start a trial of methylphenidate" for a 9-year-old while waiting for assessment. What is the right response?',o:['Agree, at a low dose','Agree if the school agrees','Explain that primary care should not start ADHD medication or diagnose ADHD; refer to the specialist service','Suggest guanfacine instead'],a:2,e:'Primary care should not make the first diagnosis or start ADHD medication in a child (NICE NG87 1.2.9). Any ADHD medicine should only be started by a professional trained and experienced in diagnosing and managing ADHD (1.7.2). A low dose, the school\u2019s support or a non-stimulant such as guanfacine changes nothing: there is no diagnosis yet, and the wrong person would be starting treatment. Explain this kindly and make sure the specialist referral is in place.'},
 {q:'An adult referral mentions "struggling since a promotion 6 months ago", with no childhood concerns. What do you do first?',o:['Book a full ADHD assessment immediately','Clarify childhood history and other explanations before accepting','Reject the referral because onset was in adulthood','Send an ASRS and diagnose if it is positive'],a:1,e:'Adult referral needs symptoms that began in childhood and persisted, are not explained by another condition, and cause at least moderate impairment (NICE NG87 1.2.10). New problems after a promotion could be stress, anxiety or low mood, so check childhood history first. Booking straight away, or diagnosing from a positive ASRS, skips that check. Rejecting outright is also wrong: an uncertain time of onset should not on its own exclude ADHD (1.3.3), and higher demands can unmask lifelong symptoms.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.2.1, 1.2.2, 1.2.4\u20131.2.11, 1.3.3, 1.7.2 (checked 21 Oct 2026).'},

{n:2,id:'prep',t:'Preparation and screening',s:'Pre-assessment questionnaires, information requests, and why a screen is only a screen.',
 vis:{svg:'prep'},
 teach:'<p>NICE NG87 1.3.1 says a diagnosis rests on a full clinical and psychosocial assessment, a full developmental and psychiatric history, <strong>observer reports</strong> and a mental state assessment. Preparation gathers those inputs before the appointment, from more than one person and more than one setting.</p>\
<h4>What to send out (adults)</h4><ul><li><strong>Self-report:</strong> the ASRS v1.1 (18 items) as a screen and a baseline for measuring treatment response, and a childhood recall scale such as the WURS-25.</li><li><strong>Impairment:</strong> the WFIRS self-report, to see which areas of life are affected.</li><li><strong>History questionnaire:</strong> development, education, work, health, family history of ADHD and other neurodevelopmental conditions.</li><li><strong>Informant forms</strong> (with consent): someone who knows the person now, and someone who knew them before 12.</li><li><strong>Coexisting conditions:</strong> PHQ-9, GAD-7, AUDIT-C and DAST-10 in every pack; the AQ-10 to screen for autism, which often coexists with ADHD. Some services use the free ACE+ v2 pack (ACE+ SR and IR) instead of separate forms.</li></ul>\
<h4>What to request</h4><ul><li><strong>School reports</strong> (the whole set) and anything written in childhood.</li><li><strong>Records:</strong> GP summary, CAMHS or paediatric letters, educational psychology reports and any previous ADHD assessment.</li></ul>\
<h4>Check before the appointment</h4><ul><li><strong>Risk:</strong> read suicide and self-harm items the day forms come back (PHQ-9 item 9) and act on them.</li><li><strong>Why now, and whose idea?</strong> Outside pressure (an employer, a course, a court) changes how you weigh self-report.</li><li><strong>Physical and cardiac history:</strong> past medical history, fainting, chest pain or breathlessness on exertion, and sudden cardiac death in the family, so the baseline in step 11 is ready.</li></ul>\
<p class="ag-note"><strong>Tests are not routine.</strong> Some local pathways ask for an ECG or blood tests with every referral. NICE does not: an ECG is only needed for a cardiac feature or a medicine with cardiac risk (NG87 1.7.4–1.7.5), and blood tests only when clinically indicated.</p>\
<h4>For children</h4><p>Parent and teacher rating scales (for example Conners or SNAP-IV), the SDQ, a school report with work samples, and the ACE v2 pre-assessment forms for 5–16-year-olds.</p>\
<h4>A screen is not a diagnosis</h4><p>The <strong>ASRS v1.1 Part A</strong> (items 1–6) is the screener. Four or more answers in the shaded boxes means symptoms are highly consistent with adult ADHD and further assessment is warranted. It does not make the diagnosis, and a negative screen does not rule ADHD out. Part B (items 7–18) has no total score. NG87 1.3.2: never diagnose on rating scales alone; they are helpful added tools.</p>\
<p>Try the ASRS screen in the <strong>Practise box</strong> at the end of this section (step 2). Items 1–3 count from <em>Sometimes</em>; items 4–6 count from <em>Often</em>. The full official wording, scoring and every other form are in the <a href="#form-library">standalone Form library</a>.</p>',
 chk:['Self-report forms sent (ASRS v1.1, childhood recall scale)','Impairment scale sent (WFIRS)','History questionnaire sent','Informant forms sent with consent (now and before 12)','Coexisting-condition screens sent (PHQ-9, GAD-7, AUDIT-C, DAST-10, AQ-10)','School reports and childhood evidence requested','GP and previous records requested','Returned forms read for risk the day they arrive','Why now and whose idea recorded','Cardiac and medical history checked (no routine ECG or bloods)','Screening result recorded as a screen only'],
 w:['asrs'],
 q:[{q:'Sam\u2019s ASRS Part A shows 5 of 6 in the shaded boxes. What does that mean?',o:['Sam has ADHD','Sam probably has ADHD and can start medication','Symptoms are highly consistent with ADHD and full assessment is warranted','The result is only valid with Part B'],a:2,e:'ASRS Part A is a 6-item screen: 4 or more answers in the shaded boxes is a positive screen, so a full assessment is warranted. A screen cannot diagnose ADHD or justify medication; diagnosis must not rest on rating scales alone (NICE NG87 1.3.2). Part A can be read without Part B; Part B adds detail to explore at interview.'},
 {q:'A teacher\u2019s rating scale is in the clinical range but the parent\u2019s is normal. What next?',o:['ADHD is excluded','ADHD is confirmed','Explore with specific examples from both, and consider observation','Only trust the teacher'],a:2,e:'Raters often disagree because settings and demands differ, so the gap is useful information, not a verdict. Neither scale alone can confirm or exclude ADHD, and trusting only the teacher ignores half the evidence (NICE NG87 1.3.2: no diagnosis on rating scales alone). Ask both for specific, recent examples, and consider observation, for example at school, when there is doubt (1.3.2). Step 12 shows how to weigh disagreement.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.3.1, 1.3.2, 1.7.4–1.7.5; NICE NG225 (self-harm); NICE CG142 (AQ-10); WHO ASRS v1.1 scoring instructions; WFIRS (Weiss, CADDRA); UKAAN AQAS 2024 (informants, records, screening for coexisting conditions); ACE/ACE+ v2 (Young, psychology-services.uk.com).'},

{n:3,id:'consent',t:'Consent and setting',s:'Explain the process, agree who you can contact, and set up the room.',
 vis:{img:'ag-room',alt:'Illustration of a child playing with blocks while a parent and clinician talk in a calm, child-friendly room'},
 teach:'<p>Start by explaining what the assessment involves, how long it takes, who you would like to speak to and what will happen with the information. Agree the person\u2019s goals for the assessment.</p>\
<h4>Consent and confidentiality</h4><ul><li>Get consent to contact informants (parents, partner, school, employer) and to see records. Record what was agreed.</li><li>Young people may be able to consent for themselves depending on their maturity and understanding (Gillick competence in the UK). Involve parents or carers as appropriate. <span class="badge unv">General UK practice; check local policy</span></li><li>Seek the child\u2019s or young person\u2019s own views in an age-appropriate way (NG87 1.3.6).</li><li>Explain the limits of confidentiality (risk to self or others, safeguarding).</li></ul>\
<h4>Time and setting</h4><ul><li>A full adult structured interview such as the DIVA-5 takes about 1 to 1.5 hours; the Young DIVA-5 about 1 hour with a parent present (DIVA Foundation). A complete assessment (history, MSE, physical, collateral) takes longer.</li><li>Use a quiet room with few distractions, allow breaks, and for children have something to do with their hands.</li><li>Watch how the person behaves throughout: restlessness, attention, interrupting. Observation during the interview is part of the evidence, but remember many people with ADHD can focus well in a novel, one-to-one setting.</li></ul>',
 chk:['Process, timing and purpose explained','Person\u2019s goals recorded','Consent for informants and records recorded','Child\u2019s or young person\u2019s views sought (NG87 1.3.6)','Limits of confidentiality explained','Room and breaks arranged'],
 w:[],
 dic:[{s:'Leo sits quietly and draws for 20 minutes in the clinic room with the clinician one-to-one.',a:0,r:'Observation',e:'Calm behaviour in clinic does not rule ADHD out. A new, interesting, one-to-one setting with an adult\u2019s full attention is exactly where many children with ADHD manage well. Base the judgement on reports of usual behaviour across settings over time; observation is only an added tool (NICE NG87 1.3.1, 1.3.2).'}],
 q:[{q:'A 15-year-old wants to be seen alone for part of the assessment. What do you do?',o:['Refuse; parents must be present throughout','Agree to see them alone for part of the time, explaining confidentiality and its limits','Cancel the assessment','See them alone and tell parents nothing whatsoever'],a:1,e:'Seeing a young person alone for part of the time is good practice: they may share things they would not say in front of parents, and NICE NG87 1.3.6 asks you to take their views into account. Explain confidentiality and its limits first, because risk and safeguarding concerns must be shared. Refusing or cancelling loses their voice. Promising to tell parents nothing at all is a promise you cannot keep if there is risk.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.3.6; DIVA Foundation (DIVA-5, Young DIVA-5 durations); general UK consent practice (Gillick).'},

{n:4,id:'pc',t:'Presenting complaint',s:'Why now, what is the problem in their own words, and what do they want?',
 vis:{img:'ag-adult-life',alt:'Illustration of an adult at a cluttered desk with sticky notes and unopened post'},
 teach:'<p>Let the person tell their story first. Use open questions, then focus. Record their own words.</p>\
<h4>A structure that works</h4><ul><li><strong>Why now?</strong> What has changed (a new job, exams, a relationship, a child\u2019s diagnosis)?</li><li><strong>What exactly happens?</strong> Ask for recent, specific examples, not labels. "Tell me about the last time\u2026"</li><li><strong>Since when?</strong> Has it always been like this, or was there a time it was different?</li><li><strong>Where?</strong> Home, school, work, friends, driving.</li><li><strong>What does it cost?</strong> Lost jobs, failed exams, debt, fines, conflict, injuries.</li><li><strong>How do they cope?</strong> Compensation (lists, alarms, a partner doing paperwork, working longer hours) can hide symptoms, especially in women and high-ability people.</li><li><strong>What do they want?</strong> Understanding, support at work or school, treatment.</li></ul>\
<p>Avoid leading questions such as "Are you easily distracted?" Instead ask "What happens to your attention in meetings?"</p>',
 chk:['Reason for referral and "why now" recorded','Problems described with specific recent examples','Duration and pattern explored (lifelong vs change)','Settings and costs recorded','Coping strategies and compensation explored','Person\u2019s goals recorded'],
 w:[],
 q:[{q:'Which question is best for exploring inattention without leading?',o:['"Do you have ADHD-type attention problems?"','"You\u2019re easily distracted, aren\u2019t you?"','"Tell me what happened the last time you had to read a long document at work."','"Score your attention from 1 to 10."'],a:2,e:'An open question about a real, recent event gets a concrete example. You can then judge it against the criteria: how often, how severe, and what it cost. Asking about "ADHD-type problems" or "You\u2019re easily distracted, aren\u2019t you?" leads the person towards a yes. A 1 to 10 score gives a number but no evidence about frequency, setting or impairment.'},
 {q:'A high-achieving adult says she has "no problems" because she works until midnight to keep up. How do you interpret this?',o:['No impairment, so not ADHD','Compensation may be hiding impairment; explore the cost of coping','She is exaggerating','She needs medication immediately'],a:1,e:'Working until midnight to keep up is a cost of coping, not proof of no impairment. Ask what happens without the strategies and what they cost in sleep, health and relationships. Reading "no problems" at face value, or calling it exaggeration, misses masking; NICE NG87 1.2.2 warns that ADHD is under-recognised in girls and women. Medication now is premature because the assessment is not finished.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.2.2; clinical interviewing practice.'},

{n:5,id:'interview',t:'Structured interview: the 18 symptoms',s:'Go through every symptom, in childhood and now, and decide whether each one counts.',
 vis:{svg:'grid'},
 teach:'<p>A structured or semi-structured interview makes sure every DSM-5-TR symptom is covered with examples. Common UK choices:</p>\
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="ag-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Who</th><th>Key points</th></tr></thead><tbody>\
<tr><td>DIVA-5</td><td>Adults</td><td>All 18 symptoms in childhood and adulthood with examples; impairment in 5 areas; informant where possible; about 1\u20131.5 h. Not free: \u20ac10 per DIVA from the DIVA Foundation.</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Young DIVA-5</td><td>5\u201317 years</td><td>About 1 h, with a parent present.</td></tr>\
<tr><td>DIVA-5 ID</td><td>Adults with intellectual disability</td><td>With carers; takes longer.</td></tr>\
<tr><td>ACE / ACE+ v2</td><td>ACE 5\u201316; ACE+ 17+</td><td>Free (Prof Susan Young); DSM-5 and ICD-11; includes presentation during the interview and co-existing problems.</td></tr>\
<tr><td>K-SADS-PL DSM-5</td><td>6\u201318 years</td><td>Semi-structured; parent and child interviewed separately, then summary ratings; ADHD in the neurodevelopmental supplement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\
<p>DIVA-5 covers core ADHD symptoms only, so a general psychiatric assessment is still needed (co-existing conditions are common).</p>\
<h4>When does a symptom count?</h4><p>For each symptom, a "yes" needs all of these:</p><ol class="ag-rules"><li><strong>Often</strong> and <strong>persistent</strong>: at least 6 months, not occasional.</li><li><strong>Beyond developmental level</strong>: more than expected for age (and intellectual level).</li><li><strong>Impairing</strong>: it directly affects social, school or work functioning, not just mildly annoying.</li><li><strong>Not better explained</strong> by anxiety, low mood, sleep, substances, another condition, opposition or not understanding.</li></ol>\
<p>Thresholds (DSM-5-TR): <strong>6 or more</strong> of 9 in a domain for children up to 16; <strong>5 or more</strong> at 17 and over. For an adult, establish <em>several</em> symptoms before 12 and the full current picture.</p>\
<p><strong>Use the interview tool</strong> in the Practise box at the end of this section (step 5). Pick a case at the top. For each symptom, read the prompt and the evidence, then decide <em>Counts</em> or <em>Doesn\u2019t count</em> in each column. Then check your answer. Open "Examples and rules" to see everyday child and adult examples.</p>',
 chk:['All 9 inattention symptoms asked, with examples','All 9 hyperactivity-impulsivity symptoms asked, with examples','Each symptom rated for childhood and now (adults) or by informant/setting (children)','Each "yes" checked: often, 6+ months, beyond developmental level, impairing, not better explained','Tally against threshold (6+ under 17; 5+ at 17+)','Informant used where possible'],
 w:['interview'],
 dic:[{s:'Adult, 25: loses keys "about once a month", like most people they know.',a:0,r:'Often / beyond normal',e:'A symptom counts only if it happens often and more than expected for the person\u2019s age. Losing keys once a month, like most peers, is ordinary forgetfulness. Ask how often, compared with whom, and what it costs (DSM-5-TR criterion A).'},
 {s:'Child, 6: cannot sit through a 40-minute school assembly, like several classmates.',a:0,r:'Developmental level',e:'Symptoms must go beyond what is expected for the child\u2019s developmental level. A 40-minute assembly is hard for many 6-year-olds, and several classmates struggle too. Compare with same-age peers and ask about other everyday situations (DSM-5-TR criterion A; NICE NG87 1.3.5 on age-adjusted criteria).'},
 {s:'Adult, 40: forgetting appointments started 3 months ago with low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest.',a:0,r:'Better explained / not persistent',e:'New forgetfulness that began 3 months ago, alongside low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest, fits depression better than ADHD. ADHD symptoms are long-standing, with several present before 12, not a recent change. Assess and treat mood first, then re-check attention (DSM-5-TR criteria B and E).'},
 {s:'Adolescent, 15: finishes friends\u2019 sentences daily; teachers and friends complain; it has been like this since primary school.',a:1,r:'Counts',e:'This counts: it happens daily, has been present since primary school, is beyond what is expected at 15, and causes problems with teachers and friends. Finishing other people\u2019s sentences is a hyperactive-impulsive symptom. Record the example and who reported it (DSM-5-TR criterion A).'}],
 q:[{q:'Sam is 34 with 8 current inattentive and 4 current hyperactive-impulsive symptoms. Which presentation fits now?',o:['Combined','Predominantly inattentive','Predominantly hyperactive-impulsive','Not enough for any presentation'],a:1,e:'From age 17, DSM-5-TR criterion A needs 5 symptoms in a domain (6 under 17). Sam\u2019s 8 inattentive symptoms meet the threshold; 4 hyperactive-impulsive symptoms fall one short. So the current presentation (past 6 months) is predominantly inattentive. Combined needs both domains met, and "not enough" is wrong because one domain is met. Still record the sub-threshold hyperactive symptoms, as they can add to impairment.'}],
 src:'DSM-5-TR Criterion A and specifiers (APA 2022; paraphrased); DIVA Foundation; ACE/ACE+ v2 (Young); Kaufman et al. 2016 (K-SADS-PL DSM-5).'},

{n:6,id:'onset',t:'Onset before age 12',s:'Show that several symptoms were present in childhood, using collateral where possible.',
 vis:{img:'ag-collateral',alt:'Illustration of old school reports tied with string, childhood drawings and a photo on a desk'},
 svg2:'timeline',
 teach:'<p>DSM-5-TR criterion B needs <strong>several</strong> inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms <strong>before age 12</strong>. ICD-11 says significant symptoms are evident before 12, typically in early to mid-childhood. This does not need a diagnosis in childhood, and does not need the full threshold before 12.</p>\
<h4>Sources of evidence</h4><ul><li><strong>Parents or older siblings</strong>: often the best source; ask for specific memories.</li><li><strong>School reports</strong>: comments such as "dreamy", "chatters", "could do better if he concentrated" at several ages are useful. Read the comments, not just the grades.</li><li><strong>Old letters</strong>, health visitor or GP records, previous assessments.</li><li><strong>The person\u2019s own recall</strong>: less reliable alone; memory of childhood is often patchy.</li></ul>\
<h4>When onset is uncertain</h4><p>NG87 1.3.3 says exclusion based on an uncertain time of onset is <strong>not recommended</strong>. If no informant is available, document what you have and weigh it carefully. Late-onset symptoms with no childhood evidence should prompt you to look for other causes (Step 13).</p>\
<p>Late compensation is common: a bright child in a structured school may cope until secondary school, university or work removes the structure. Symptoms were still there; impairment appeared when demands rose.</p>',
 chk:['Childhood symptoms explored with specific examples','Informant account (parent/sibling) where possible','School reports reviewed','Earliest age of symptoms recorded','Uncertain onset documented, not used alone to exclude (NG87 1.3.3)'],
 w:[],
 dic:[{s:'Adult, 28: no informant available, but school reports at 7 and 10 say "can\u2019t sit still, talks constantly, rarely finishes work".',a:1,r:'Onset',e:'School reports at ages 7 and 10 describe several symptoms before 12, which meets criterion B even without an informant. Records written at the time are often stronger evidence than memory. Note the source and dates in the report (DSM-5-TR criterion B).'},
 {s:'Adult, 45: says attention was "perfect" until a head injury at 40; mother confirms no childhood problems.',a:0,r:'Onset / better explained',e:'Normal attention until a head injury at 40, confirmed by the mother, shows onset in adulthood after a brain injury. That fits an acquired brain injury, not ADHD, which is neurodevelopmental and starts in childhood. Arrange assessment of the cognitive effects of the injury (DSM-5-TR criteria B and E).'},
 {s:'Student, 19: coped well at school, struggling at university. Parents recall homework battles, losing things and needing constant reminders from age 8.',a:1,r:'Onset',e:'Parents recall homework battles, losing things and needing constant reminders from age 8, so several symptoms were present before 12. School structure and parental support kept things manageable. Impairment often shows later, when that support goes, for example at university. Coping well at school does not rule out childhood onset (DSM-5-TR criterion B).'},
 {s:'Adult, 30: no informant, no records, vague recall of childhood. Current symptoms are clear and impairing.',a:0,r:'Onset uncertain',e:'Onset is not yet established: there is no informant, no records and only vague recall. But an uncertain time of onset should not on its own exclude ADHD (NICE NG87 1.3.3). Look for more evidence, such as school reports, old letters or another relative, and record clearly what is and is not known.'}],
 q:[{q:'What does DSM-5-TR criterion B need?',o:['A childhood diagnosis','Full criteria met before 12','Several symptoms present before 12','Symptoms before 7'],a:2,e:'Criterion B needs several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms before age 12. It does not need full criteria to have been met then, or a childhood diagnosis: many adults were never assessed as children. "Before 7" was the DSM-IV rule; DSM-5 raised the age to 12, partly because exact onset is hard to recall in adulthood.'}],
 src:'DSM-5-TR Criterion B (paraphrased); ICD-11 6A05 (MMS 2026-01); NICE NG87 1.3.3.'},

{n:7,id:'settings',t:'Two or more settings',s:'Check that several symptoms appear across home, school, work and social life.',
 vis:{img:'ag-observation',alt:'Illustration of a classroom where one child looks out of the window and another is out of their seat'},
 svg2:'settings',
 teach:'<p>DSM-5-TR criterion C needs several symptoms in <strong>two or more settings</strong> (for example home, school or work, with friends or relatives, or other activities). NG87 1.3.3 says symptoms should be pervasive, happening often in <strong>two or more important settings</strong> including social, familial, educational or occupational settings. ICD-11 notes that symptoms vary with the structure and demands of a setting.</p>\
<h4>How to establish it</h4><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> parent and teacher reports; a school observation can help when there is doubt (NG87 1.3.2). Clubs, grandparents and childminders add evidence.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> work, home, studies, social life, driving and money. A partner, friend or colleague can describe what they see.</li></ul>\
<h4>One setting only?</h4><p>If problems are only at school, think about the classroom, learning difficulties, bullying or a poor relationship with the teacher. If only at home, think about family stress, parenting, sleep, screens or trauma. See Step 13.</p>',
 chk:['Home','School / work / study','Social','Other (clubs, driving, money)','Informant for each setting where possible','Observation arranged if doubt (NG87 1.3.2)'],
 w:['settings'],
 dic:[{s:'Child, 9: very disruptive at school, but calm and focused at home, at Cubs and with grandparents.',a:0,r:'Settings',e:'Problems in one setting only do not meet the settings rule: ADHD needs symptoms in 2 or more settings (DSM-5-TR criterion C; NICE NG87 1.3.3). Calm, focused behaviour at home, at Cubs and with grandparents points to something about school. Check learning level, the class, bullying and the relationship with the teacher.'},
 {s:'Adult, 31: disorganised at work (manager reports) and at home (partner reports unpaid bills and lost keys).',a:1,r:'Settings',e:'This counts: disorganisation at work and at home, each described by a different, independent informant (manager and partner). That meets the requirement for symptoms in 2 or more settings (DSM-5-TR criterion C; NICE NG87 1.3.3). Record a specific example from each.'}],
 q:[{q:'A teacher reports severe inattention; parents see none. What is the most useful next step?',o:['Diagnose based on the teacher','Exclude ADHD','Arrange an observation and gather specific examples from both settings','Repeat the rating scales in a year'],a:2,e:'When reports conflict, observation is useful if there is doubt about symptoms (NICE NG87 1.3.2), and specific examples from each setting show what is really happening. Diagnosing on the teacher\u2019s view alone ignores the need for symptoms in 2 or more settings (1.3.3); excluding ADHD ignores the teacher. Repeating scales in a year delays help and adds no new information.'}],
 src:'DSM-5-TR Criterion C (paraphrased); NICE NG87 1.3.2, 1.3.3; ICD-11 6A05.'},

{n:8,id:'impair',t:'Functional impairment',s:'Show how the symptoms affect daily life, domain by domain.',
 vis:{svg:'radar'},
 teach:'<p>Symptoms without impairment are not a disorder. DSM-5-TR criterion D needs clear evidence that symptoms interfere with or reduce the quality of social, academic or occupational functioning. NG87 1.3.3 needs at least <strong>moderate</strong> psychological, social and/or educational or occupational impairment, based on interview and/or direct observation.</p>\
<h4>Domains to cover</h4><ul><li>Family and relationships</li><li>School or work (learning, performance, behaviour, jobs lost)</li><li>Life skills (money, driving, self-care, sleep, time management)</li><li>Self-concept (self-esteem, feeling a failure)</li><li>Social (friendships, being excluded)</li><li>Risk (accidents, injuries, driving offences, substances, sexual risk, offending)</li></ul>\
<p>The DIVA-5 covers impairment in 5 areas (work or education, relationships or family, social contacts, free time or hobbies, self-confidence or self-image). The WFIRS (Weiss Functional Impairment Rating Scale) rates items 0\u20133 over the last month; a domain is impaired if 2 or more items score 2, or 1 item scores 3, or the domain mean is 1.5 or more.</p>\
<p><strong>Try the impairment rater</strong> in the Practise box at the end of this section (step 8) (example items are paraphrased, not the WFIRS wording).</p>',
 chk:['Family/relationships','School/work','Life skills (money, driving, self-care, sleep)','Self-concept','Social','Risk (injuries, driving, substances, offending)','Overall impairment at least moderate (NG87 1.3.3)'],
 w:['wfirs'],
 dic:[{s:'Adult, 29: always late for social events and friends tease them about it, but nothing else is affected.',a:0,r:'Impairment',e:'Teasing about lateness is mild annoyance, not the at least moderate impairment that ADHD needs (NICE NG87 1.3.3). Nothing else is affected: no lost friendships, jobs or money. Note the symptom, but it does not show impairment (DSM-5-TR criterion D).'},
 {s:'Child, 10: has no friends at school because he interrupts and grabs; is behind in reading; daily family conflict at homework.',a:1,r:'Impairment',e:'This counts: no friends because of interrupting and grabbing (social), behind in reading (education) and daily conflict over homework (family). That is clear impairment in several domains (DSM-5-TR criterion D; NICE NG87 1.3.3). Also check whether the reading delay has another cause, such as a specific learning difficulty.'}],
 q:[{q:'Using the WFIRS rule, a domain has items rated 1, 1, 2, 0, 3. Is it impaired?',o:['Yes','No','Only if the mean is 1.5 or more','Cannot tell'],a:0,e:'Under the CADDRA WFIRS rule, a domain is impaired if 2 or more items score 2, or any item scores 3, or the domain mean is 1.5 or more. One item here is rated 3, so the domain is impaired. The mean is only 1.4 (7 over 5 items), which is why relying on the mean alone would wrongly say no. Always confirm the score with real examples at interview.'}],
 src:'DSM-5-TR Criterion D (paraphrased); NICE NG87 1.3.3; DIVA Foundation; WFIRS (Weiss; CADDRA scoring instructions; Canu 2018).'},

{n:9,id:'history',t:'Background history',s:'Developmental, psychiatric, medical, family, substance and forensic history.',
 vis:{svg:'wheel'},
 teach:'<p>NG87 1.3.4 asks you to include an assessment of the person\u2019s needs, coexisting conditions, social, familial and educational or occupational circumstances and physical health, and, for children and young people, the mental health of their parents or carers.</p>\
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="ag-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What to ask</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>\
<tr><td>Pregnancy and birth</td><td>Alcohol and drugs in pregnancy, prematurity, birth complications</td><td>Preterm birth (NG87 1.2.1); FASD (NICE QS204)</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Development</td><td>Milestones, speech, motor skills, early social communication</td><td>Learning disability, autism, language disorder</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Education and work</td><td>Schools, exclusions, exams, jobs, dismissals</td><td>Evidence of impairment and of childhood symptoms</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Psychiatric</td><td>Mood, anxiety, psychosis, eating disorders, self-harm, previous diagnoses and treatment</td><td>Differentials and coexisting conditions; SmPC contraindications</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Medical</td><td>Epilepsy, head injury, sleep, thyroid, hearing and vision, cardiac symptoms</td><td>Mimics and safety before treatment (NG87 1.7.4)</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Prescribed, over the counter, supplements</td><td>Mimics (akathisia, sedation) and interactions</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Family</td><td>ADHD and other conditions in relatives; sudden cardiac death; parents\u2019 mental health</td><td>Heritability; cardiac risk (NG87 1.7.5); NG87 1.3.4</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Substances</td><td>Alcohol, cannabis, stimulants, caffeine</td><td>Mimics; misuse and diversion risk (NG87 1.7.23\u20131.7.24)</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Forensic and social</td><td>Offending, driving offences, housing, debt, safeguarding</td><td>Impairment and risk; justice system (NG87 1.2.1)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>',
 chk:['Pregnancy and birth (alcohol, prematurity)','Developmental milestones','Education and employment history','Psychiatric history and risk','Medical history (epilepsy, head injury, sleep, thyroid, cardiac)','Medicines','Family history (ADHD, sudden cardiac death, parental mental health)','Substance use','Forensic and social history, safeguarding'],
 w:[],
 q:[{q:'Which family history item changes the physical-health plan before any ADHD medication?',o:['A cousin with ADHD','A parent with anxiety','Sudden death of a sibling at 30 from a heart problem','A grandparent with type 2 diabetes'],a:2,e:'Sudden death of a first-degree relative under 40 that suggests heart disease is one of the reasons in NICE NG87 1.7.5 to get a cardiology opinion before starting ADHD medication. It may point to an inherited heart condition. A cousin with ADHD, a parent with anxiety and a grandparent with type 2 diabetes are worth recording, but none of them changes the cardiac plan.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.2.1, 1.3.4, 1.7.4, 1.7.5, 1.7.23, 1.7.24.'},

{n:10,id:'mse',t:'Mental state examination',s:'What you see and hear in the room, and the features that point elsewhere.',
 vis:{svg:'mse'},
 teach:'<p>The MSE records what you observe now. In ADHD it is often unremarkable apart from restlessness and distractibility. Its main value is to pick up features that point to <strong>other</strong> conditions.</p>\
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="ag-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Common in ADHD</th><th>Points elsewhere</th></tr></thead><tbody>\
<tr><td>Appearance and behaviour</td><td>Fidgeting, getting up, picking at things</td><td>Psychomotor slowing (depression), agitation (mania, akathisia), tics</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Speech</td><td>Talkative, interrupts, goes off topic but returns</td><td>Pressured, hard to interrupt (mania); poverty of speech</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Mood and affect</td><td>Usually reactive; may be frustrated or low because of impairment</td><td>Persistent low mood, elation, flat affect</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Thought form</td><td>Tangential, jumps between topics</td><td>Flight of ideas (mania), loosening of associations (psychosis)</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Thought content</td><td>Frustration, self-criticism</td><td>Suicidal ideation, persistent worries, obsessions, delusions</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Perception</td><td>Normal</td><td>Hallucinations</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Cognition</td><td>Distractible; may need repetition</td><td>Disorientation, marked memory impairment</td></tr>\
<tr><td>Insight</td><td>Often good</td><td>Poor insight in psychosis or mania</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\
<p>Always ask about suicidal thoughts and self-harm, and document risk.</p>',
 chk:['Appearance and behaviour','Speech','Mood and affect','Thought form and content','Perception','Cognition','Insight','Risk assessment documented'],
 w:[],
 q:[{q:'During interview, an adult speaks rapidly, cannot be interrupted, jumps between loosely linked ideas, and says they have slept 3 hours a night all week without feeling tired. What is the priority?',o:['Complete the ADHD interview','Assess for hypomania or mania and manage risk before proceeding','Diagnose combined ADHD','Send a WFIRS'],a:1,e:'Pressured speech, flight of ideas and little need for sleep without tiredness suggest hypomania or mania. This needs urgent assessment and risk management, and ADHD symptoms cannot be judged reliably during a mood episode (DSM-5-TR criterion E). Carrying on, sending a WFIRS or diagnosing ADHD would miss a possible emergency. It also matters for treatment: mania and poorly controlled bipolar disorder are contraindications in the methylphenidate SmPC (section 4.3), and NICE NG87 1.7.19 says stop ADHD medication during an acute manic or psychotic episode.'}],
 src:'MSE: standard psychiatric practice. NICE NG87 1.7.19; Concerta XL SmPC 4.3.'},

{n:11,id:'physical',t:'Physical baseline',s:'Height, weight, heart rate and blood pressure, cardiac history, and when an ECG is needed.',
 vis:{img:'ag-physical',alt:'Illustration of a nurse measuring a child\u2019s blood pressure with a child-sized cuff while a parent sits beside them'},
 teach:'<p>A physical baseline belongs in every assessment where medication may follow. NG87 1.7.4 (before starting medication) asks for: mental health and social circumstances, a review of physical health including medical history and medicines, height and weight (measured and recorded against the normal range for age, height and sex), <strong>baseline pulse and blood pressure</strong> (with an appropriately sized cuff, compared with the normal range for age), and a cardiovascular assessment. It also asks for a review to confirm the person still meets ADHD criteria and a risk assessment for substance misuse and drug diversion. An <strong>ECG is not needed</strong> before stimulants, atomoxetine or guanfacine unless the person has any of the features in 1.7.5 or a coexisting condition being treated with a medicine that may pose an increased cardiac risk.</p>\
<h4>NG87 1.7.5: refer for a cardiology opinion before starting medication if any of these apply</h4><ul><li>a history of congenital heart disease or previous cardiac surgery</li><li>a history of sudden death in a first-degree relative under 40 suggesting a cardiac disease</li><li>shortness of breath on exertion compared with peers</li><li>fainting on exertion or in response to fright or noise</li><li>palpitations that are rapid, regular and start and stop suddenly (fleeting occasional bumps are usually ectopics and need no investigation)</li><li>chest pain suggesting cardiac origin</li><li>signs of heart failure</li><li>a murmur heard on cardiac examination</li><li>blood pressure classified as hypertensive for adults</li></ul>\
<p>NG87 1.7.6: refer to a paediatric hypertension specialist before starting medication if blood pressure is consistently above the 95th centile for age and height for children and young people.</p>\
<p><strong>Practise the decision</strong> with the baseline tool in the Practise box at the end of this section (step 11). The full lesson, with the ECG-or-cardiology decision tool and the baseline checklist, is <a href="#m3">Module 3: Before treatment, the baseline</a>.</p>',
 chk:['Height and weight (centiles for children)','Pulse and BP with the right cuff size, against the normal range for age','Cardiovascular history and examination','1.7.5 features screened','ECG not needed unless 1.7.5 features or cardiac-risk co-medication','Paediatric hypertension referral if BP consistently >95th centile (1.7.6)'],
 w:['baseline'],
 q:[{q:'An 11-year-old has no cardiac symptoms, no relevant family history and a normal examination. Is a routine ECG needed before methylphenidate?',o:['Yes, always','No, not unless a 1.7.5 feature or a cardiac-risk co-medication is present','Only if the parent asks','Only for doses above 36 mg'],a:1,e:'NICE NG87 1.7.4: an ECG is not needed before stimulants, atomoxetine or guanfacine unless there is a 1.7.5 feature (for example fainting on exertion, a murmur, or sudden cardiac death in a first-degree relative under 40) or a coexisting condition treated with a medicine that may raise cardiac risk. This child has none. A parent\u2019s request or the planned dose does not by itself call for an ECG. Still record pulse, blood pressure, height and weight at baseline (1.7.4).'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.7.4, 1.7.5, 1.7.6 (checked 21 Oct 2026).'},

{n:12,id:'collateral',t:'Collateral and informant disagreement',s:'Bringing together different accounts, and what to do when they differ.',
 vis:{svg:'informants'},
 teach:'<p>Collateral history from someone who knows the person well is one of the most valuable parts of an ADHD assessment. Informants often notice things the person does not (and vice versa).</p>\
<h4>Why informants disagree</h4><ul><li><strong>Different settings</strong>: a structured classroom versus a relaxed home, or the opposite.</li><li><strong>Different expectations</strong>: what counts as "a lot" varies between people and cultures.</li><li><strong>Different knowledge</strong>: a new teacher, a partner of a few months, a parent who works long hours.</li><li><strong>Informant factors</strong>: stress, their own mental health (NG87 1.3.4 asks about parents\u2019 mental health), or a wish for a particular outcome.</li><li><strong>Self-report limits</strong>: adults with ADHD may under-report impairment; some people over-report.</li></ul>\
<h4>How to resolve it</h4><ol><li>Ask each informant for <strong>specific recent examples</strong>, not ratings.</li><li>Compare like with like: is it the same symptom, in the same setting, over the same period?</li><li>Seek a <strong>third source</strong> (another teacher, records, a club leader).</li><li>Consider an <strong>observation</strong> when there is doubt (NG87 1.3.2).</li><li>Record the disagreement and how you weighed it.</li></ol>',
 chk:['Informant account(s) obtained','Disagreements listed','Specific examples sought','Third source considered','Observation considered if doubt','Weighing of evidence documented'],
 w:['disagree'],
 q:[{q:'An adult rates themselves as severely impaired at work, but the manager reports no concerns and good appraisals. What do you do?',o:['Accept the self-report','Accept the manager\u2019s report','Explore specific examples, the cost of coping, and other explanations; seek further sources','Stop the assessment'],a:2,e:'Both views can be biased: self-report can over- or under-rate, and a manager may see only the finished work. Ask for specific examples, the hidden cost of coping (working late, help from colleagues) and other explanations such as anxiety or low mood, and seek further sources such as a partner or records. Accepting one report, or stopping, throws away evidence; a full assessment includes observer reports (NICE NG87 1.3.1).'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.3.2, 1.3.4; clinical practice.'},

{n:13,id:'ddx',t:'Differential diagnosis',s:'Rule in or out each condition that can mimic ADHD, step by step.',
 vis:{svg:'tree'},
 teach:'<p>DSM-5-TR criterion E: symptoms do not occur only during schizophrenia or another psychotic disorder and are not better explained by another mental disorder (for example mood, anxiety, dissociative or personality disorder, substance intoxication or withdrawal). ICD-11 also excludes symptoms better accounted for by another disorder or due to a substance or medication.</p>\
<p>Two questions for every alternative:</p><ol><li><strong>Is it a mimic?</strong> Does it explain the symptoms instead of ADHD?</li><li><strong>Is it a co-traveller?</strong> Many conditions co-exist with ADHD and both can be diagnosed.</li></ol>\
<p>Clues that point away from ADHD: onset in adolescence or adulthood with no childhood evidence; an episodic course; symptoms in one setting only; symptoms tied to worry, trauma, sleep, substances or a medical event.</p>\
<p><strong>Work through the decision tree</strong> in the Practise box at the end of this section (step 13), then open each card for what to ask, what to test, and how to tell it apart. Use "Run with case" to see the answers for Sam or Leo.</p>',
 chk:['Anxiety','Depression','Bipolar disorder','Sleep disorders (incl. OSA)','Thyroid disease','Substances and medicines','Trauma, PTSD, attachment','Learning disability / specific learning difficulty','Autism','FASD','Personality disorder (EUPD)','Hearing and vision','Epilepsy (absences)','ODD, conduct, tics','Acquired brain injury / other medical'],
 w:['ddx'],
 q:[{q:'A 7-year-old has "daydreaming" episodes several times a day lasting a few seconds, with eyelid fluttering. He cannot be roused during them. What next?',o:['Diagnose predominantly inattentive ADHD','Refer for specialist assessment for possible absence seizures','Start parent training','Reassure'],a:1,e:'Several brief staring spells a day, with eyelid fluttering, a sudden start and end, and no response during them, suggest absence seizures. ADHD daydreaming can usually be interrupted. Diagnosing ADHD, starting parent training or reassuring would miss a treatable neurological condition. Refer for specialist assessment (NICE NG217, epilepsies).'},
 {q:'An adult with autism has lifelong inattention and impulsivity in all settings. Can ADHD be diagnosed?',o:['No, autism excludes ADHD','Yes, if criteria are met; NG87 1.3.3 says do not exclude because of a pervasive developmental disorder','Only after age 25','Only if the autism is mild'],a:1,e:'Autism does not exclude ADHD: NICE NG87 1.3.3 says not to exclude ADHD because of a pervasive developmental disorder, and DSM-5-TR allows both diagnoses together. Age and autism severity are not rules. Do check that the symptoms are not better explained by autism alone, for example inattention only to topics that do not interest the person. If ADHD is treated, titrate more slowly and monitor more often (1.7.28).'}],
 src:'DSM-5-TR Criterion E (paraphrased); ICD-11 6A05; NICE NG87 1.2.1, 1.3.3; condition-specific NICE guidance on each card.'},

{n:14,id:'comorb',t:'Coexisting conditions: what to address first',s:'A safe order of priorities when ADHD comes with other problems.',
 vis:{svg:'ladder'},
 teach:'<p>Coexisting conditions are the rule rather than the exception. The question is not only "is it ADHD?" but "what needs to be addressed first, and what can go in parallel?"</p>\
<p>The ladder in the Practise box (step 14) is a teaching summary. Each rung cites its source; where no guideline sets the order, it is labelled clinical practice.</p>',
 chk:['Immediate risk addressed (suicide, self-harm, safeguarding)','Psychosis or mania excluded or treated','Severe depression or eating disorder stabilised','Substance misuse plan (preparation choice if misuse/diversion risk)','Cardiac issues assessed','Anxiety, autism, tics: same medication choices; slower titration may be needed','Learning and social support in parallel'],
 w:['treatfirst'],
 q:[{q:'An adult with ADHD also has active psychotic symptoms. What is right?',o:['Start a stimulant at a low dose','Treat the psychosis first; stimulants are contraindicated with psychotic symptoms','Start atomoxetine','Offer both at once'],a:1,e:'Active psychosis comes first. Stimulants can worsen psychosis, and psychotic symptoms and schizophrenia are contraindications in the methylphenidate SmPC (section 4.3). NICE NG87 1.7.19 says stop ADHD medication during an acute psychotic or manic episode and consider restarting once it has resolved. A low dose, a switch to atomoxetine or treating both at once all ignore the active psychosis.'},
 {q:'A child with ADHD also has anxiety. What does NG87 say about medication choice?',o:['Do not treat ADHD','Offer the same medication choices as for others, and consider slower titration and more frequent monitoring','Use guanfacine only','Use a benzodiazepine first'],a:1,e:'NICE NG87 1.7.18: offer the same medication choices to people with ADHD and anxiety, tics or autism as to anyone else with ADHD. Because anxiety is present, titrate more slowly and monitor more often (1.7.28). Not treating ADHD denies effective care, guanfacine is not required, and benzodiazepines do not treat ADHD.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.7.18, 1.7.19, 1.7.23, 1.7.24, 1.7.28; Concerta XL SmPC 4.3.'},

{n:15,id:'formulate',t:'Formulation and coding',s:'Bring it together: criteria A to E, presentation, severity and the ICD-11 code.',
 vis:{svg:'gates'},
 teach:'<p>Check each criterion in turn. All five must be met.</p><ol class="ag-rules"><li><strong>A</strong> Symptoms: 6+ (5+ at 17+) in inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity, for 6+ months, beyond developmental level, with direct impact on functioning.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Several symptoms before 12.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Several symptoms in 2+ settings.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Clear evidence of impairment.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Not better explained by another condition.</li></ol>\
<h4>Presentation (based on the past 6 months)</h4><ul><li><strong>Combined</strong>: both A1 (inattention) and A2 (hyperactivity-impulsivity) met.</li><li><strong>Predominantly inattentive</strong>: A1 met, A2 not.</li><li><strong>Predominantly hyperactive-impulsive</strong>: A2 met, A1 not.</li></ul>\
<h4>Severity (DSM-5-TR, paraphrased)</h4><ul><li><strong>Mild</strong>: few if any symptoms beyond those needed, and no more than minor impairment.</li><li><strong>Moderate</strong>: between mild and severe.</li><li><strong>Severe</strong>: many symptoms beyond those needed, or several particularly severe symptoms, or marked impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>In partial remission</strong>: full criteria met before, fewer than full criteria for the past 6 months, with continuing impairment.</p>\
<h4>ICD-11 codes</h4><p>6A05.0 predominantly inattentive; 6A05.1 predominantly hyperactive-impulsive; 6A05.2 combined; 6A05.Y other specified; 6A05.Z unspecified. ICD-11 has no symptom count: the code is a clinical judgement about which pattern predominates, not a conversion of DSM-5-TR tallies. NG87 1.3.3 asks for symptoms to meet DSM-5 or ICD-11 criteria. NG87 1.3.5: use age-adjusted criteria.</p>\
<p><strong>The formulation tool</strong> in the Practise box (step 15) uses your Step 5 answers for the selected case.</p>',
 chk:['Criterion A tallies recorded','B onset documented','C settings documented','D impairment documented','E alternatives considered','Presentation specified','Severity specified','ICD-11 code recorded'],
 w:['formulate'],
 q:[{q:'A 12-year-old has 7 inattentive and 3 hyperactive-impulsive symptoms meeting all other criteria. Which ICD-11 code?',o:['6A05.2','6A05.0','6A05.1','6A05.Z'],a:1,e:'6A05.0 is ICD-11 predominantly inattentive presentation. Using DSM-5-TR counts, 7 inattentive symptoms meet the threshold of 6 under age 17, and 3 hyperactive-impulsive symptoms do not, so inattention predominates. 6A05.2 is combined (both domains), 6A05.1 is predominantly hyperactive-impulsive and 6A05.Z is unspecified. ICD-11 itself has no symptom count, so the code records a clinical judgement of the main pattern.'}],
 src:'DSM-5-TR criteria A\u2013E, presentation and severity specifiers (paraphrased); ICD-11 6A05 (MMS 2026-01); NICE NG87 1.3.3, 1.3.5.'},

{n:16,id:'decide',t:'The diagnostic decision',s:'ADHD, not ADHD, or not yet: and what to do in each case.',
 vis:{svg:'fork'},
 teach:'<p>There are three honest outcomes:</p><ul><li><strong>ADHD diagnosed</strong>: all criteria met, with sufficient evidence. Record presentation, severity and coexisting conditions.</li><li><strong>Not ADHD</strong>: criteria not met, or symptoms better explained by something else. Say what you think explains the difficulties and what help is available. A "no" should still leave the person with a plan.</li><li><strong>Uncertain / more information needed</strong>: for example no childhood evidence yet, an untreated mood or sleep disorder clouding the picture, or conflicting reports. Say exactly what is needed (school reports, observation, informant, treatment of the other condition then re-assess) and when you will review.</li></ul>\
<p>Avoid diagnosing on a single source, on a positive screen alone (NG87 1.3.2), or under time pressure. If you are unsure, discuss with your supervisor or the MDT.</p>',
 chk:['Decision recorded (ADHD / not ADHD / uncertain)','Reasoning against each criterion','If uncertain: what is needed and by when','If not ADHD: alternative explanation and help','Discussed with supervisor/MDT where needed'],
 w:['decide'],
 q:[{q:'An adult has clear current symptoms and impairment, but no informant and no records. Low mood is moderate and untreated. What is the best decision?',o:['Diagnose ADHD','Exclude ADHD','Uncertain: treat or stabilise mood, seek childhood evidence, and re-assess','Prescribe a stimulant and see'],a:2,e:'Onset is not established (no informant, no records), and untreated moderate low mood can cause or worsen poor concentration (DSM-5-TR criterion E). That makes the decision uncertain, not negative: do not exclude ADHD on uncertain onset alone (NICE NG87 1.3.3). Diagnosing or prescribing now would be premature. Treat or stabilise mood, look for childhood evidence, then re-assess.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.3.2, 1.3.3; clinical practice.'},

{n:17,id:'report',t:'Writing the report',s:'A clear report the person, GP and school can use.',
 vis:{svg:'report'},
 teach:'<p>A good report explains how you reached the decision, not just the decision. Write in plain English where possible and send a copy to the person (and parents or carers as appropriate). With consent, share relevant parts with school or college and other professionals (NG87 1.4.12, 1.4.13).</p>\
<h4>Suggested structure</h4><ol><li>Reason for referral and sources of information</li><li>Presenting difficulties (in the person\u2019s words)</li><li>Symptom review: childhood and current, with tallies</li><li>Onset, settings and impairment</li><li>Background history</li><li>Mental state and physical baseline</li><li>Differential diagnosis: what was considered and why it was ruled in or out</li><li>Formulation and diagnosis (DSM-5-TR and ICD-11), presentation, severity, coexisting conditions</li><li>Recommendations and plan</li><li>Risk and safety-netting</li></ol>\
<p><strong>The report template in the Practise box (step 17) fills in from the selected worked case.</strong> Copy or print it, and adapt it to your service\u2019s template.</p>',
 chk:['Sources listed','Symptom tallies with examples','Criteria A\u2013E addressed','Differentials documented','Diagnosis with presentation, severity, ICD-11 code','Plan and recommendations','Copy to person (and GP); school with consent'],
 w:['report'],
 q:[{q:'Who should get a copy of an adult\u2019s ADHD assessment report?',o:['Only the GP','The person, and with their consent the GP and others involved','The employer automatically','Nobody until treatment starts'],a:1,e:'The report is about the person\u2019s own care, so they should get a copy. Sharing it with the GP and others involved needs their consent, except where safeguarding or serious risk requires sharing. Sending it only to the GP leaves the person out, an employer never gets it automatically, and holding it back until treatment delays information the person needs (professional confidentiality standards and UK GDPR).'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.4.12, 1.4.13; clinical practice.'},

{n:18,id:'feedback',t:'Feedback and next steps',s:'Explaining the outcome and setting up the NG87 pathway.',
 vis:{img:'ag-feedback',alt:'Illustration of a clinician explaining an outcome to a parent and a young person at a round table'},
 teach:'<p>Feedback is where the person starts to make sense of the diagnosis. Allow time, check understanding and give written information.</p>\
<h4>After a diagnosis: NG87 1.4.3 structured discussion</h4><p>Have a structured discussion with the person (and their family or carers as appropriate) about how ADHD could affect their life. NG87 lists, for example: the positive impacts of a diagnosis (understanding symptoms, building on strengths, access to services); the negative impacts (stigma and labelling); a greater tendency for impulsive behaviour; the importance of environmental modifications; education issues (reasonable adjustments at school and college); employment issues (career choices, rights to reasonable adjustments); social relationships; the challenges when there are coexisting conditions; the increased risk of substance misuse and self-medication; and the possible effect on driving (people with ADHD must declare their diagnosis to the DVLA if their symptoms or medication affect their ability to drive safely). This discussion should inform the shared treatment plan.</p>\
<h4>School and other professionals</h4><p>With consent, contact the school or college (NG87 1.4.12) and other professionals involved (1.4.13).</p>\
<h4>The NG87 treatment pathway in brief</h4><ul><li><strong>Children 5+ and young people:</strong> ADHD-focused information and support, environmental modifications; medication only if symptoms still cause persistent significant impairment in at least one domain after environmental modifications have been implemented and reviewed (1.5.13). Methylphenidate is first-line (1.7.7).</li><li><strong>Under 5:</strong> ADHD-focused group parent training first (1.5.7).</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> medication if symptoms still cause significant impairment in at least one domain after environmental modifications (1.5.15); lisdexamfetamine or methylphenidate first-line (1.7.11).</li></ul><p>See Module 4 (treatment pathway) and Module 5 (titration).</p>\
<h4>If ADHD was not diagnosed</h4><p>Explain what you think is happening, what help is available, and how to access it. Signpost and refer as needed.</p>',
 chk:['Outcome explained in plain language','NG87 1.4.3 topics covered (incl. driving/DVLA)','Written information given','School/college contacted with consent (1.4.12)','Other professionals informed with consent (1.4.13)','Next steps on the NG87 pathway agreed','If not ADHD: alternative explanation and signposting'],
 w:[],
 q:[{q:'Which topic is specifically listed in NG87 1.4.3 for the post-diagnosis discussion?',o:['Choice of brand','Driving, including the need to tell the DVLA for some people','The person\u2019s IQ','Genetic testing'],a:1,e:'NICE NG87 1.4.3 lists the possible effect on driving: symptoms can impair driving, medication may improve it, and people must tell the DVLA if their symptoms or medication affect their ability to drive safely. The list also covers the positives and negatives of diagnosis, changes to the environment, education, work, relationships, coexisting conditions and the risk of substance misuse. Brand choice, IQ and genetic testing are not on it.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.4.3, 1.4.12, 1.4.13, 1.5.7, 1.5.13, 1.5.15, 1.7.7, 1.7.11.'},

{n:19,id:'limits',t:'Safety netting and limits of competence',s:'Who can diagnose, learning under supervision, and keeping people safe.',
 vis:{svg:'scope'},
 teach:'<div class="ag-scope" role="note"><strong>This guide supports, and does not replace, supervised training.</strong> NG87 1.3.1: a diagnosis of ADHD should only be made by a specialist psychiatrist, paediatrician or other appropriately qualified healthcare professional with training and expertise in diagnosing ADHD, on the basis of a full clinical and psychosocial assessment, a full developmental and psychiatric history, and observer reports and assessment of mental state. NG87 1.7.2: ADHD medication should only be started by a healthcare professional with training and expertise in diagnosing and managing ADHD.</div>\
<h4>Learning to assess safely</h4><ul><li>Observe experienced assessors, then assess with supervision, then present cases to your supervisor before giving outcomes.</li><li>Work within your service\u2019s protocols and your professional regulator\u2019s standards on competence.</li><li>NG87 1.1.7: trusts should ensure specialist ADHD teams develop age-appropriate training programmes for professionals. NG87 1.1.8: psychiatrists, paediatricians and other mental health professionals should undertake training so that they can diagnose and manage ADHD in line with the guideline.</li></ul>\
<h4>Safety netting</h4><ul><li>Document risk (suicide, self-harm, risk to others, safeguarding) and act on it.</li><li>Tell people what to do if things get worse while waiting: who to contact, crisis numbers.</li><li>Driving: discuss safety; people must tell the DVLA if symptoms or medication affect safe driving (NG87 1.4.3).</li><li>If you are unsure, say so, seek advice and arrange follow-up.</li></ul>',
 chk:['Working within competence and service protocol','Supervisor or MDT involved','Risk documented and acted on','Safety-net advice given (who to contact, crisis)','Driving discussed','Follow-up arranged'],
 w:[],
 q:[{q:'You are a prescriber new to ADHD. A friend asks you to "quickly diagnose" their partner. What is right?',o:['Do a quick ASRS and diagnose','Decline; advise a GP referral to a specialist service','Diagnose informally but don\u2019t prescribe','Prescribe a trial dose'],a:1,e:'ADHD should only be diagnosed by an appropriately qualified specialist after a full assessment (NICE NG87 1.3.1), and a prescriber new to ADHD does not yet have that expertise. A quick ASRS is only a screen (1.3.2), an informal diagnosis still labels the person, and a trial dose breaks the rule that only a trained specialist starts ADHD medication (1.7.2). Assessing friends or family also blurs professional boundaries, so advise a GP referral.'}],
 src:'NICE NG87 1.1.7, 1.1.8, 1.3.1, 1.4.3, 1.7.2.'}
];
